nodecode-lcm-x is a rewrite of electricsheephq's electricsheephq/lcm-x:

  https://github.com/electricsheephq/lcm-x
  commit 984a40045372f1f24a87d060194786f4d1843b90
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Stephen Schoettler (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the promise and the verbs. The original hooks a Hermes turn's
before-call, after-call and subagent points and keeps every message and tool
result in an embedded SQLite store, compressing older turns into a tree of
summaries that the model can walk back down into the verbatim text. This cell
keeps every turn the same way -- the operator's ask, the answer and each
tool result, verbatim, on the :frame point -- in one append-only JSON file per
session, and gives the model the four verbs that matter over it: lcm_search
finds the blocks carrying a query's words, lcm_expand reads them (and their
neighbours) back in full by the reference lcm:<session>:<seq>, lcm_recent
lists the newest, and lcm_status says what is held and where. /lcm and
(help :lcm-x) are the operator's side of it. The references, the
lossless store and the "search, then read back verbatim" flow are the
original's; the SQLite DAG, the summary tree and the tool names are not.

Left out, and why: the DAG of summaries and its compaction policy, the
embedding provider and its semantic search, the retrieval planning, the
evidence compiler and the diagnostic and doctor verbs (16 tools in all). Each
of them compresses or re-ranks context so that a smaller window suffices --
which is the Hermes harness's problem, not Nodecode's, where the model edits
its own working context and a whole turn costs nothing to keep. What is kept
is the half Nodecode does not already have: a store outside the window that
outlives compaction and answers the exact words rather than a summary of them.
Search is lexical (every word of the query must appear) instead of embedded,
so there is no model call, no key and no server.
